CAT = "auwhiteRisk"; SEG = "RSK"
Q = [
("In WHS terms, what is a hazard?",
 "Anything that could cause harm", ["The chance that harm happens", "An injury that has occurred", "A rule for doing work safely"],
 "A hazard is a situation or thing with the potential to harm a person, such as an unguarded edge or a live lead. Risk is the chance of that harm happening and how bad it could be."),
("In WHS terms, what is risk?",
 "Likelihood and severity of harm", ["Anything with potential to harm", "A list of the site's rules", "A report of a past incident"],
 "Risk combines how likely it is that a hazard will cause harm and how serious that harm could be. The hazard itself is the thing that could cause the harm."),
("What is the first step of the five-step risk management process?",
 "Identify the hazard", ["Assess the risk", "Control the hazard", "Review the controls"],
 "The unit sets five steps in order: identify the hazard, assess the risk, consult and report, control the hazard, review. You cannot assess or control a hazard nobody has found."),
("In the five-step risk management process, what comes straight after assessing the risk?",
 "Consult and report", ["Identify the hazard", "Review the controls", "Control the hazard"],
 "The order is identify, assess, consult and report, control, review. Reporting before controlling makes sure the right people decide on the control."),
("What is the last step of the five-step risk management process?",
 "Review the controls", ["Assess the risk", "Consult and report", "Identify the hazard"],
 "Review checks that the controls are working and have not created new hazards. It closes the loop back to identifying hazards."),
("Which control measure is the most effective?",
 "Eliminating the hazard", ["Wearing PPE", "Putting up a warning sign", "Training the workers"],
 "Elimination sits at the top of the hierarchy of controls because the hazard is gone. Signs, training and PPE rely on people behaving correctly every time."),
("Which control measure is the least effective and used only as a last resort?",
 "PPE", ["Isolation", "Substitution", "Engineering"],
 "Personal protective equipment is at the bottom of the hierarchy because the hazard is still there and protection depends on correct fit and use. It is used with higher controls or when they are not practicable."),
("Swapping a solvent-based paint for a water-based one is an example of:",
 "Substitution", ["Elimination", "Isolation", "An admin control"],
 "Substitution replaces a hazard with something less hazardous. The paint job still happens, so the hazard is reduced rather than eliminated."),
("Fencing off an area so nobody can get near a hazard is an example of:",
 "Isolation", ["Substitution", "Elimination", "PPE"],
 "Isolation separates people from the hazard by distance or a physical barrier. The hazard is still there, so it ranks below elimination and substitution."),
("Fitting a dust extraction shroud to a concrete saw is an example of:",
 "An engineering control", ["An administrative control", "Substitution", "Elimination"],
 "Engineering controls are physical changes to plant or processes, such as extraction, guarding or ventilation. They work without relying on people remembering a rule."),
("Rotating workers to limit their time on a vibrating tool is an example of:",
 "An administrative control", ["An engineering control", "Eliminating the hazard itself", "Isolation"],
 "Administrative controls are work methods, procedures and training, such as job rotation and time limits. They rely on people following them, so they rank low in the hierarchy."),
("Assembling roof trusses on the ground instead of at height is an example of:",
 "Elimination", ["Isolation", "Substitution", "PPE"],
 "Doing the work on the ground removes the fall hazard for that task completely, which is elimination, the most effective control."),
("What is a job safety analysis (JSA) used for?",
 "Listing a task's steps and hazards", ["Recording a worker's daily hours", "Ordering materials for the job", "Notifying the regulator of injury"],
 "A JSA breaks a task into steps, identifies the hazards in each step and sets a control for each one. It is used for tasks that are not high risk construction work."),
("When must a safe work method statement (SWMS) be prepared?",
 "Before high risk construction work", ["For every task on site, however small", "Only after an incident", "Only on jobs over a month"],
 "A SWMS is required before high risk construction work starts. Other tasks may use a JSA or similar, but a SWMS is the legal requirement for high risk construction work."),
("What does a safe work method statement (SWMS) describe?",
 "Hazards, risks and controls", ["Workers' pay rates and hours", "The building's design loads", "Who owns the plant on site"],
 "A SWMS identifies the high risk construction work, the hazards and risks it creates, and how the control measures will be put in place, monitored and reviewed."),
("High risk construction work is not being done the way its SWMS says. What must happen?",
 "Stop the work until it complies", ["Finish, then update the SWMS", "Continue but tell the client", "Continue if someone watches"],
 "If high risk construction work is not being carried out in line with the SWMS, it must stop immediately, or as soon as it is safe to stop, and only resume when it complies."),
("Which of these is high risk construction work?",
 "Work with a fall risk over 2 m", ["Using a hand drill at bench height", "Digging a trench 1 m deep", "Painting from a 1 m platform"],
 "Construction work involving a risk of a person falling more than 2 metres is high risk construction work and needs a SWMS. A 1 m trench is below the 1.5 m trigger."),
("A trench becomes high risk construction work once it is deeper than:",
 "1.5 metres", ["1 metre", "2.5 metres", "3 metres"],
 "Work in or near a shaft or trench deeper than 1.5 metres, or a tunnel, is high risk construction work, so a SWMS is required."),
("Which of these is also high risk construction work?",
 "Work next to a road in use", ["Tiling a ground-floor bathroom", "Painting an inside wall", "Laying carpet in an office"],
 "Construction work on or next to a road, railway or other traffic corridor in use is high risk construction work. The other tasks are ordinary low-level construction work."),
("Who must make sure a SWMS is prepared before high risk construction work starts?",
 "The PCBU doing the work", ["The safety regulator", "The local council", "The building's owner or client"],
 "The PCBU carrying out the high risk construction work must make sure a SWMS is prepared, or already exists, before the work starts, and must consult the workers involved."),
("Who should be consulted when a SWMS is prepared?",
 "The workers doing the task", ["Only the client and architect", "Only the building inspector", "Only the local council"],
 "Workers who will do the work know its real hazards, so consultation with them is required. Their input makes the SWMS practical rather than a paper exercise."),
("A risk matrix rates a risk by combining:",
 "Likelihood and consequence", ["Cost and time to fix", "Location and weather", "Crew size and the hours worked"],
 "A risk matrix plots how likely harm is against how severe it would be, giving a rating such as low, medium, high or extreme. The rating shows which risks to control first."),
("When should control measures be reviewed?",
 "After an incident or change", ["Only once a year", "Only at project end", "Only if the regulator asks for it"],
 "Controls must be reviewed when they are not working, after an incident, before a change to the workplace, when a new hazard is found, or when workers or an HSR ask."),
("No single control removes a risk completely. What should be done?",
 "Combine several controls", ["Accept the risk as it is", "Rely on PPE by itself", "Move on to the next task"],
 "Controls are often combined, for example an engineering control backed by safe work procedures and PPE. Relying on PPE alone is the weakest option."),
]
